# sys_clock_syscall.tkr — the §16 THIRD-CRUMB fixture. `main.tks` owns a caller-allocated
# `ts::Timespec` local, bridges its address to an `i64` word via `teko::sys::ref_word`, and issues
# `teko::sys::syscall2(sys::SYS_CLOCK_GETTIME, sys::CLOCK_MONOTONIC, <word>)` — the raw Linux
# `clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts)` syscall. Unlike `sys_write`/`sys_exit_group`, this crumb
# reads DATA the kernel wrote THROUGH the pointer back out of the struct (`ts.sec`), proving the
# `ref_word` bridge (§1.2) plus the C1 header-less `extern type = struct` layout together produce a
# real, ABI-correct struct-pointer round-trip. The harness observes exit code 0, which only happens
# when the syscall reports success (`r == 0`) AND the monotonic clock's seconds field came back
# positive (true on any running system).

Feature: sys_clock_syscall — the §16 ref_word bridge + syscall2 intrinsic issue Linux clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts)

  Scenario: raw_clock_gettime_fills_struct_and_exits_zero — the process reads the monotonic clock through a struct pointer and exits 0
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
